** Map Interface :-

java.util
 ├── Collection (Interface)
 │    ├── List (Interface)
 │    ├── Set (Interface)
 │    └── Queue (Interface)
 └── Map (Interface)
       ├── HashMap (Class)
       ├── LinkedHashMap (Class)
       ├── TreeMap (Class)
       └── Hashtable (Class)


Actually,Map is part of the Java Collections Framework,but it is not considered a direct Collection.Here’s why:

The Collection interface is the root of the collection hierarchy for storing individual objects.
Map is part of the java.util package, but it does not extend Collection. Instead,
it is a separate interface used for key-value pair mappings.

Key Points about Map:

- Key-Value Structure: Each key maps to exactly one value.
- Unique Keys: A map cannot have duplicate keys, but it can have duplicate values.

